      cursor: `Move slow work out of this app's request path into a queue: use the infrastructure already in the repo first (if Postgres is present, a jobs table with status, attempts and available_at is a legitimate queue; if Redis or QStash is configured, use it) - do not add a new service without my approval. Producer side: the endpoint enqueues a small, versioned task message and responds. Consumer side: a worker pulls with a visibility timeout, processes exactly-once-ish via idempotency keys, retries with exponential backoff to a dead-letter state after N attempts, and logs per task. Show the table or queue schema and the worker loop.`,
      claude: `Act as a systems designer. Compare three queue tiers for a solo Next.js app: (1) a Postgres jobs table polled by a cron worker - honest, boring, enough until it is not; (2) Redis with visibility timeouts (Upstash style) - real queue semantics, one new service; (3) a managed HTTP queue like QStash - the queue calls your endpoints. For each: delivery guarantees, retry story, and the trigger to move up the ladder. State the rule that the task message stays tiny (an id, not the payload) so re-processing is re-reading current truth. Under 20 lines.`,
      chatgpt: `My app emails 5,000 users when a user posts - and the request times out doing it. I keep reading about job queues and workers. What is the queue actually, physically? Why can the web handler just 'put it in the queue' and reply instantly, and what stops the work being lost or done twice?`,
